Markdown's [vocab/markdown/CODE-BLOCK](#vocab/markdown/CODE-BLOCK) marker uses `kind: fenced` (see [Marker Kinds](#Marker Kinds)). Open is a run of three or more backticks at line start; close is a same-or-longer run on its own line. First word of the info string becomes the `language-X` class on the inner `<code>` (CommonMark / GFM convention). The rest of the info string — when present — is parsed by TW's macro-parameter attribute parser and emitted as `data-*` attributes on the outer `<pre>` (MkDocs / Hugo style metadata: `title="..."`, `highlight="..."`, etc.). See [Example - Markdown code block - with metadata](#Example - Markdown code block - with metadata).

The marker wraps the inner element in a `<pre>` and puts the marker identity classes (`.wltc-md-codeblock wltc`) on the OUTER `<pre>` — so the universal `.wltc` vertical margin lands outside the visible block, not as whitespace inside it.

## Patterns

- [Example - Markdown code block - basic](#Example - Markdown code block - basic). The conventional ` ```lang ` shape with a language identifier.
- [Example - Markdown code block - bare fence](#Example - Markdown code block - bare fence). No info string — block renders without a language class.
- [Example - Markdown code block - longer fences](#Example - Markdown code block - longer fences). 4+ backticks so the body can contain shorter backtick runs (useful when the code block itself documents other code blocks).
- [Example - Markdown code block - multi-paragraph](#Example - Markdown code block - multi-paragraph). Blank lines inside the fenced body don't terminate the block.
- [Example - Markdown code block - with metadata](#Example - Markdown code block - with metadata). MkDocs / Hugo style — `title="src/foo.py"`, `highlight="2,5"`, etc. after the language identifier; each lands as a `data-*` attribute on the outer `<pre>`.

## How the matching works

At parse time the engine:

1. At line start, looks for a maximal run of three or more fence chars (backtick or tilde) — call its length `N`. The rest of the open line (no further fence chars allowed) is the info string.
2. Scans forward, line by line, for the FIRST line whose only content is a run of `N` or more fence chars. That's the close.
3. If no such close exists in the source, the block is implicitly closed at EOF (CommonMark behaviour).
4. Body is the text between the open line's newline and the close line. Emitted verbatim as a single text node.
5. The info string's first word gets the `info-prefix` prepended and is emitted on the configured `info-attribute` on the INNER `<code>` (giving the CommonMark `language-X` class). When `info-attrs: yes`, the AFTER-first-word remainder is parsed via TW's macro-parameter attribute parser and each `name=value` (or `name:value`) emits as `data-{name}` on the OUTER `<pre>`. Marker identity classes (`.wltc-md-codeblock wltc`) stay on the OUTER `<pre>` so the universal `.wltc` margin lands outside the block.

## DSL flexibility (illustrative)

`kind: fenced` is generic — any vocab can pick its own fence character, minimum run length, and inner/outer element shape. One pattern the framework supports but doesn't ship:

### Pandoc-style admonitions / callouts

```
title: vocab/docs/CALLOUT
kind: fenced
open: ::::
element: div
info-attribute: class
info-prefix: callout-
classes: .wltc-callout
```

Usage `::::warning … ::::` → `<div class="wltc-callout wltc callout-warning">…</div>`. CSS colours by `.callout-warning` / `.callout-info` / `.callout-tip`. No `wrapper-element` since `<div>` is the natural container for block content.

## When NOT to use `fenced`

If the body needs inline parsing — wikitext, other markers, recursive structure — `kind: fenced` is the wrong choice (body is always verbatim text). Use a `word`-kind block marker with `mode: block` + an `end-string` or `paragraph-marker` instead. Reserve `fenced` for verbatim, raw-body content where the open-and-matching-close shape genuinely fits.

